                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose capabilities and the cost-control map`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Map Maasvliet's value chain on a <strong>control-vs-cost grid</strong>. R&D, BMS software and the cell-balancing IP sit top-right: high cost, but they are the <strong>source of your pricing power</strong>. Back-office, power-electronics assembly, testing and after-sales are context — good global-sourcing candidates. The whole exercise turns on one classification: in 2019 you offshored a <em>core, IP-bearing</em> activity to chase a saving, defects and a design leak followed, and you reversed it in 2022. Name that lesson honestly now, because the board will test it on every round that follows. Cost is not the only axis.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the global-sourcing footprint`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `You must recover <strong>≥ EUR 45M a year</strong>. Set the <strong>location</strong> axis (onshore / nearshore / offshore) and, independently, the <strong>governance</strong> axis (captive vs. outsourced) — these are <em>two decisions, not one</em>. Offshore Asia gives the deepest saving but the most geopolitical exposure; a nearshore mix is tariff-light and Mexico serves the US lane. Keep the cell-balancing / BMS work off every flagged border and out of third-party hands, or you repeat 2019. And do not hit the number through a <strong>single concentrated lane</strong>: one export-control listing and the whole plan stalls — dual-source the main relocated lane.`,
