                    coach_default:          `Decision recorded.`,
                    coach_r1_core:          `<strong>Right call — the cell-balancing IP is core.</strong> It is the reason your gross margin exists; you keep it in-house regardless of the saving. That is the single classification 2019 got wrong. Hold it through the next three rounds.`,
                    coach_r1_context:       `<strong>You just called your crown jewels “assembly work.”</strong> The cell-balancing IP is the source of your pricing power — classify it as context and you have set up 2019 to happen again the moment a big saving appears. Cost is not the only axis.`,
                    coach_r1_unsure:        `<strong>“Decide on price alone” is exactly the 2019 reasoning.</strong> If you treat the IP like any other cost line, the biggest saving will always point at offshoring it. Match location to <em>criticality</em>, not just to cost.`,
                    coach_r1_candall:       `<strong>Putting “everything with a saving” in the candidate set means the cell-balancing assembly is on the table.</strong> That is the precise move that leaked the design in 2019. Context activities are the candidates; core, IP-bearing work is not.`,
                    coach_r1_candnone:      `<strong>Keeping the entire value chain onshore is safe — and it closes none of the EUR 45M gap.</strong> The board needs the cost recovered without repeating 2019, not a freeze. Offshore the <em>context</em> activities; protect only the core.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Core IP kept in-house, only context activities flagged for sourcing, and the 2019 lesson named as a criticality error rather than a vendor problem. That is the control-vs-cost map the board needs — carry it into the footprint decision.`,
                    coach_r1_vendor:        `<strong>“A better vendor fixes it” misreads 2019.</strong> The failure was not the contract manufacturer — it was offshoring a <em>core, IP-bearing</em> activity at all. Swap the vendor and you still hand the design to a third party across a flagged border.`,
                    coach_r1_luck:          `<strong>“Bad luck, no real lesson” is the answer that gets you 2019 again.</strong> The leak was a predictable consequence of the location/criticality mismatch. If there is no lesson, there is no defence when the board asks why this time is different.`,

                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A workable footprint.</strong> Now check it against all three constraints at once: did you clear EUR 45M, keep the IP off flagged borders and out of third-party hands, and avoid a single concentrated lane? Location and governance are two levers — use both.`,
                    coach_r2_ipleak:        `<strong>You just moved cell-balancing work offshore for the saving.</strong> That is the same class of decision as 2019 — your gross-margin advantage comes from that IP, and the design copy compresses margin 6–8 points within two years. EUR 12M of saving against your entire pricing power. What stops the copy this time?`,
                    coach_r2_repeat2019:    `<strong>Offshore AND outsourced — that is 2019 reconstructed.</strong> Core IP, across a flagged border, run by a third party: every axis that failed last time, set up again. Pull the cell-balancing work back in-house and onshore before the board sees this.`,
                    coach_r2_dual:          `<strong>Dual-sourcing the main lane is the resilient move.</strong> It costs a little unit-cost margin, but one export-control listing no longer stalls the whole plan. Savings with a second source beats a slightly cheaper plan with none.`,
                    coach_r2_single:        `<strong>You found the saving through one offshore hub in a flagged jurisdiction.</strong> Savings achieved, resilience zero — one listing and the whole footprint stops. Where is your second source? Resilience is part of the sourcing decision, not an afterthought.`,
                    coach_r2_deepfast:      `<strong>Offshoring deep and fast through one lane stacks the risk.</strong> The quality-ramp lengthens and the tariff exposure piles onto a single jurisdiction. Phase it, or split the lane — the headline saving is not the realised saving if the ramp slips.`,

                    coach_r3_export:        `<strong>Export is fast and cheap — until policy reprices it.</strong> A tariff swing adds 6–10% to landed cost and you fail the domestic-content test the incentives require. Did you model the policy, or only the freight?`,
                    coach_r3_license:       `<strong>Licensing is capital-light and fast — and it leaks the technology.</strong> You would hand a US partner the very BMS know-how you refused to offshore in Round 2. Capital saved, control gone.`,
                    coach_r3_jv:            `<strong>A JV shares the cost and risk and qualifies for incentives.</strong> The trade is diluted control and a partner you do not fully govern — protect the IP in the JV terms, and watch the export-control exposure on the partner.`,
                    coach_r3_greenfield:    `<strong>A wholly-owned plant maximises control and qualifies for every incentive — and opens in ~30 months.</strong> Your USD 90M pipeline is live now and your competitor exports today. Control has a speed-and-capital cost; how do you serve the market in the next two quarters?`,
                    coach_r3_bridged:       `<strong>Bridge then build — that is how control survives a live pipeline.</strong> Export or JV serves the USD 90M now while the greenfield plant comes up, so you do not strand the pipeline waiting 30 months for maximum control.`,
                    coach_r3_reflex:        `<strong>The control reflex just stranded a live pipeline.</strong> A greenfield plant with no bridge opens in 30 months while the USD 90M is on the table now — your competitor exports today and takes it. Control beats speed only if something serves the market in the meantime.`,
                    coach_r3_policyblind:   `<strong>Cheapest mode, policy unmodelled.</strong> Export looks light until the tariff adds 9% to landed cost and you miss the domestic-content threshold the incentives require. Your “cheap” mode just became uncompetitive.`,
                    coach_r3_ignorepolicy:  `<strong>“Incentives and tariffs are noise” is how the cheap mode becomes the expensive one.</strong> Domestic-content rules can flip the economics toward local presence entirely — price the policy, not just the logistics.`,
                    coach_r3_stranded:      `<strong>You over-committed capital to a light entry mode.</strong> Heavy capital into export or a license strands cash without buying the control or content fit a plant or JV would. Match the commitment to the mode.`,

                    coach_r4_contingency:   `<strong>The contingency held.</strong> Because Round 2 left you a second lane and the IP off flagged borders, the tariff hike and the listing shift you to the fallback instead of stalling the plan. That is resilience as a design choice, not a hope.`,
                    coach_r4_nocontingency: `<strong>You “activated a contingency” you never built.</strong> Round 2 left a single concentrated lane and exposed IP — there is no second source to shift to. The shock lands on a plan with no give. The contingency has to exist before the shock, not after.`,
                    coach_r4_absorb:        `<strong>Absorbing the shock eats 6–10% of landed cost and the delay.</strong> “The plan holds on paper” is the 2019 sentence — it held on paper then too. A plan that only survives if nothing moves is not resilient.`,
                    coach_r4_rebuild:       `<strong>Re-planning live means there was never a contingency.</strong> Rebuilding the footprint under fire costs time you do not have with the renewal lock and the pipeline both live. Resilience is built into Round 2, not improvised in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r4_savingsled:    `<strong>Leading with the EUR 45M alone is what 2019 did.</strong> The number was right last time too — and the IP leak still cost 6–8 margin points. Trade savings against control and resilience explicitly, or the board hears 2019.`,
                    coach_r4_controlled:    `<strong>Control-only is safer but it leaves the pipeline and the cost gap on the table.</strong> The customers are demanding the cut and the USD 90M is live — an integrated plan reconciles all four KPIs, not just the one that scares the board.`,
                    coach_r4_rebuttal:      `<strong>That rebuttal lands because the plan earns it.</strong> IP in-house, off flagged borders, with a named second source — different from 2019 on every axis that failed. That is the answer to “you sound like 2019.”`,
                    coach_r4_hollow:        `<strong>You said it is different from 2019 — but the plan repeats it.</strong> The IP went offshore or the lane is a single flagged hub. The board lived through this once; the rebuttal is hollow until the footprint actually changes.`,
                    coach_r4_cheaper:       `<strong>“Cheaper than 2019, so the upside outweighs the risk” is the 2019 argument verbatim.</strong> Cost was never the axis that failed — control was. The board will not buy the same trade with a new price tag.`,
                    coach_r4_trust:         `<strong>“Trust me, the team is more experienced” is not a control mechanism.</strong> The board needs a structural answer — where the IP sits, who runs it, what the second source is — not a promise. That is exactly what was said in 2019.`,

